        ],
        "review": false
      },
      "pace-repl-pty-warmup-by-executed-marker": {
        "title": "Pace REPL PTY warm-up by executed markers",
        "text": "For native REPL latency tests, send many warm-up entries in bounded batches. End each batch with a unique printed marker constructed so the literal marker never appears in the echoed command. Wait for that marker before sending the next batch. This limits PTY input pressure and confirms that the REPL completed the warm-up before latency measurement starts.",
        "children": [],
        "sources": [
          "src/go/kame/cmd/kame/repl_terminal_linux_test.go#TestNativeInteractiveKashPersistentLatency"
        ],
        "review": false
      },
      "preserve-callable-ownership-in-process-outcomes": {
        "title": "Preserve callable ownership across outcomes and bindings",
        "text": "Keep returned lexical closures in evaluator-owned process outcomes, outside process wire metadata and engine-published values. Core Clone and CloneOwned copy value storage without retaining callable wrappers. Use the evaluator's callable borrowing and release helpers when copying or freeing an outcome, and retain async closure snapshots to the end of their script group. Verify the returned closure still works in a fresh scope after capture, command-list selection, and repeated waits.\n\nWhen changing Kash let or set with returned closures, test a binding that owns a closure capturing its descendant scope, including container values and replacement. In the lifetime test, retain an external callable borrow, release the initial scope owners, and verify both scopes stay reachable. Release the last borrow and verify that both scopes clear. Separately invoke a returned callable after rebinding and host suspension.",
        "children": [],
        "sources": [
