      "run-native-cli-tests": {
        "title": "Run native CLI tests explicitly",
        "text": "When verifying changes to the native CLI or interactive Kash tests, build the CLI and set KAME_NATIVE_CLI_TEST_BINARY to its absolute path before running the package tests; otherwise those tests silently skip and a passing plain go test is incomplete. For the full cmd/kame suite, build the test binary in a supported artifact mode (for example, go build -ldflags=-X=main.artifactMode=debug); ordinary go build defaults to development and causes TestHelpAndVersion to fail its required build-mode check.",
        "children": [],
        "sources": [
          "identity==\"kame:observations\" item=99608bfc-677e-4332-bb77-d8cf859aabac",
          "identity==\"kame:observations\" item=k-85274f569b38b5beecf2ac6c2fb3465238a7bae006fc3a5cff9367bbf6409aa7",
          "identity==\"kame:observations\" item=k-bb34a976707a2d8238f646f2100f3baf6710f4bac5a0e2392d1dc901063938e1"
        ],
        "review": false
      },
      "test-absent-and-invalid-host-edge-telemetry": {
        "title": "Test absent and malformed host edge telemetry separately",
        "text": "When changing process graph telemetry, exercise both absence and malformed data at the WASM host completion boundary. Remove optional edge metrics and verify unavailable measurements with null counters. Then corrupt a row, counter, or measurement kind and verify that HOST_FAIL reaches the caller even through an inspected process outcome. Keep the ordinary terminal graph parity checks alongside these protocol tests.",
        "children": [],
        "sources": [
          "tests/T042-07-kash-metrics.sh",
          "src/go/kame/lang/eval/process_metrics.go"
        ],
        "review": false
      },
      "test-all-interactive-hook-success-paths": {
        "title": "Test all interactive hook success paths",
        "text": "When adding or changing a success hook in interactive polling, verify both the poll-completion path and any successful early-return path. Ordinary interactive statements are marked Definition, so a Definition with a current node can return before Poll and leave a hook exercised only on the Poll path untested.",
        "children": [],
        "sources": [
          "identity==\"kame:observations\" item=k-7f92012b5e03fc56a58ef9c1be0181f5e5e29791bd0485014ddbae5202ad4f57"
        ],
        "review": false
      },
      "test-cross-module-computed-includes": {
        "title": "Test computed includes across modules",
        "text": "When validating native execution or evaluator changes against SDK modules, include an integration test where a computed include in one module references a definition from another module; module-isolation and literal-composition tests can miss declaration-loop scope differences.",
        "children": [],
        "sources": [
          "identity==\"kame:observations\" item=k-f87c55847a142602811e01b1e139bcfa4f280e72a2212c4e3506db06869e7c1f"
        ],
        "review": false
      },
      "validate-composed-lexical-context": {
        "title": "Preserve evaluation context during source composition",
        "text": "When a static Kash check needs bindings or captures from earlier source fragments, use syntax-only parsing during fragment discovery and validate the composed unit before effects. Test both the valid cross-fragment reference and rejection before effects through the native and WASM entry points, since their preflight paths can differ.\n\nWhen changing Kame source loaders, evaluate a conditional during composition only if it selects included sources at the loader's scope. Leave other conditionals for declaration registration so they retain its evaluation authority. A declaration-local include stays with its enclosing block and is registered in each reached iteration scope even if the source read is shared. Test declaration-phase discovery, ordinary conditional includes, and two iterations that include the same file but use different bindings through the native and WASM paths when supported. When testing list contributions across declaration-local includes, read the same list in a condition between includes and in a recipe registered before the later contribution. This checks both the reached prefix during registration and the final list the recipe consumes.\n\nTest include path validation separately from the source origin. An inline source has no base for a relative include, but it can include an absolute or home-relative path. Pair those successful inline cases with an inline relative rejection, and separately check that an invalid included Kash module reports DEF_INVALID before effects.\n\nWhen testing conditional include selection, place the authored source in a different directory from the evaluation cwd. Under CLI `-C`, verify that a relative path expression in a condition uses the effective working directory while the selected relative include still resolves beside its authoring file.\n\nWhen an early include condition uses `files`, exercise the read-grant check and verify that its membership observation reaches both registration and watch. Add a conflicting declared output and assert overlap at the authored discovery span. Then start watch with a selected include missing and change only discovery membership to make another branch viable. The build should recover without editing the source, and ordinary content reads and effects should still be rejected in conditions.\n\nWhen testing kind-filtered discovery, replace a file with a directory at the same path and verify watch reselects the branch. This exercises kind-sensitive invalidation while the unfiltered path set stays fixed. Check overlap separately with a directory-only scan and an absent matching output. In the embedding test, assert that the forwarded scan carries a plain pattern and a separate kind mask.",
        "children": [],
        "sources": [
