      "vary-graph-depth-and-registration-order-in-performance-tests": {
        "title": "Vary graph depth and registration order in performance tests",
        "text": "When validating cached-topology or prerequisite-repair performance, benchmark deep graphs under both prerequisite-first and consumer-first registration, in addition to wide and fixed-locality cases. Registration order can expose quadratic batched height repair that size/width benchmarks miss; keep a regression benchmark for the reversed order.",
        "children": [],
        "sources": [
          "identity==\"kame:observations\" item=k-53754b489ca7b546d9bd74d7e8c4436e3d8281817ce87862d4e8de6eafe6a61e"
        ],
        "review": false
      },
      "verify-borrowed-stdin-handoff-and-cancellation": {
        "title": "Preserve callable stdin descriptor handoff",
        "text": "When changing callable stdin, test both handoff directions between a subprocess and a framed reader. Check the exact unread tail, including when the delimiter arrives after multiple transport chunks. Exercise cancellation with a FIFO whose writer stays open: after a subprocess consumes one byte, let a sibling fail while the callable waits for a record. Require the reader and callable cleanup to finish before closing the writer. Close the active reader before joining the callable.\n\nIn the Node host, keep the inherited pipe descriptor free from eager Socket reads. Opening a Socket early can prefetch bytes the subprocess should receive. When Node needs a nonblocking FIFO reader, use an independent descriptor and lend the blocking descriptor to subprocesses. On Go, spawn can make the shared descriptor blocking; poll with a bounded wait before a framed read so cancellation can still wake it. Verify nested subprocess inheritance and transfers larger than the pipe buffer on both hosts.\n\nFor an invocation timeout test on a blocked FIFO read, budget time for WASM startup and require a marker emitted immediately before the read as well as the deferred cleanup marker and timeout diagnostic. Keep the FIFO writer open and use a separate harness deadline, so a timeout before the read or a stuck cleanup cannot satisfy the fixture.",
        "children": [],
        "sources": [
          "src/go/kame/host/posix/stage_io.go",
          "src/go/kame/lang/eval/function_stage.go",
          "src/go/kame/lang/eval/kash_record.go",
          "src/go/kame/lang/eval/process.go",
          "src/js/kame.js",
          "tests/T048-09-kash-stdin-records.sh",
          "tests/T055-02-kash-callable-stages.sh"
        ],
        "review": false
      },
      "verify-changed-inputs-through-indirect-consumers": {
        "title": "Test changed-inputs through an imported helper",
        "text": "When testing preparation of the changed-inputs snapshot, put its only reference in an imported helper invoked inside an async capture. A direct recipe reference can cause the snapshot to be prepared even if indirect consumers are missed. Verify a first build, a changed normal input, and a forced build with no content changes.",
        "children": [],
        "sources": [
          "src/go/kame/cmd/kame/execution_native_test.go",
          "src/go/kame/program/newer_inputs.go",
          "src/go/kame/lang/eval/kash_module.go",
          "plan-055.md"
        ],
        "review": false
      },
      "verify-child-output-drains-after-exit": {
        "title": "Verify child output drains after exit under backpressure",
        "text": "When changing native child output ownership, block the downstream sink after the first write, let the child emit a final chunk, and verify that it is reaped while the sink stays blocked. Hold the sink past the post-exit pipe timeout, release it, and assert exact output including the final chunk. Keep child reaping independent of the output sink. Bound idle post-exit reads while allowing buffered bytes to drain after sink backpressure clears.",
        "children": [],
        "sources": [
          "src/go/kame/host/posix/command_output_test.go#TestChildOutputDrainsAfterExitUnderBackpressure",
          "src/go/kame/host/posix/command_output.go",
          "tests/T012-03-streams-large-output.sh",
          "tests/T012-02-streams-live-cli.sh"
        ],
        "review": false
      },
      "verify-cli-grant-policy-frontends": {
        "title": "Verify CLI grant policy across frontends",
        "text": "When changing CLI grant policy that separates user opt-out from language-derived defaults, test the native frontend and a freshly built WASM frontend, covering both JSON options and source selection. This catches frontend drift in how those defaults and opt-outs are propagated.",
        "children": [],
        "sources": [
          "identity==\"kame:observations\" item=k-b38c22a41cdb93451b08ba5c7529119ece8edf3357058d1e55c32c078e5de405"
        ],
        "review": false
      },
      "verify-command-probes-in-kash-loop-fixtures": {
        "title": "Verify command probes in migrated Kash loop fixtures",
        "text": "When migrating a Kash loop fixture whose condition used to run a command, verify the new header inspects an explicit process outcome such as `while $?(./probe).ok`. A legacy `while ./probe` still parses as a truthy path and can hang the harness. Use an external timeout around the CLI invocation and assert the probe ran and the loop terminated.",
        "children": [],
        "sources": [
          "tests/T046-01-kash-scripting.sh",
          "docs/spec/055-unified-kame-kash-language.md",
          "docs/spec/013-tests.md"
        ],
        "review": false
      },
      "verify-deliberate-exit-at-sync-capture-boundary": {
        "title": "Verify deliberate exit at the synchronous capture boundary",
        "text": "When changing deliberate exit handling in synchronous Kash captures, exercise both text capture and inspected outcomes. Have a command-list member emit a prefix, a later member exit after registering a defer that emits a second marker, and a skipped member carry a forbidden marker. Assert that the capture retains the prefix and cleanup bytes in order, reports the appropriate text or status, and lets the caller emit a continuation marker. Consume the exit at the synchronous capture unit boundary after cleanup.\n\nInspect a direct pipeline and a pipeline reached in a command list to verify that exit handling preserves per-stage status and terminal graph metadata. Also test an ordinary deferred failure that remains inspectable and a later cleanup CAP_DENIED after an ordinary primary failure. The combined denial must still stop the caller, leave stdout empty, and retain both diagnostic codes.",
        "children": [],
        "sources": [
          "tests/T048-04-kash-exit.sh",
          "tests/T048-05-kash-with.sh",
          "tests/T055-02-kash-callable-stages.sh",
          "src/go/kame/lang/eval/execution_unit.go",
          "src/go/kame/lang/eval/kash_command_capture.go",
          "src/go/kame/lang/eval/kash_list.go",
          "src/go/kame/lang/eval/function_stage.go",
          "src/go/kame/lang/eval/process.go",
          "docs/spec/055-unified-kame-kash-language.md",
          "src/go/kame/lang/eval/defer.go",
          "src/go/kame/lang/eval/kash_exit.go",
          "src/go/kame/lang/eval/process_expression.go",
          "docs/spec/049-process-inspection.md"
        ],
        "review": false
      },
      "verify-expected-tools-besides-host-parity": {
        "title": "Check expected tools in both inspection modes",
        "text": "When changing tool inspection, exercise both global do tools and dependency-scoped do tools check. Assert expected tool names and missing-tool diagnostics in addition to comparing native and WASM output: parity can pass when both omit tools. Include a default Kash body without shell metadata and an explicit x/ command head. Verify the name is reported without its namespace and that a missing tool on a reached dependency carries the selected target stack.\n\nIn `kame do plan`, report statically referenced tools for the selected plan in a dedicated text section and a top-level JSON `tools` array. Report each tool once with its first required execution stage, using one-based stage numbers. Keep tool lists out of individual producer records. Verify the section and the tool names and stage numbers on native and WASM with T037-01.",
        "children": [],
        "sources": [
          "tests/T010-13-wasm-tools.sh",
          "src/go/kame/program/tools.go",
          "src/go/kame/program/tool_declarations.go",
          "docs/spec/037-build-inspection.md",
          "tests/T037-01-cli-build-inspection.sh"
        ],
        "review": false
      },
      "verify-fairness-with-one-ready-slot": {
        "title": "Verify engine fairness with one ready slot",
        "text": "When changing engine dispatch, keep Ready and Step on the same resource-key rotation. Exercise a one-slot dispatch while a generator keeps becoming ready and another node must complete its parent graph. A scheduler that always picks the first ready key can keep the generator running and starve completion and cleanup.",
        "children": [],
        "sources": [
          "src/go/kame/core/engine.go",
          "src/go/kame/program/materialize.go",
          "tests/T055-02-kash-callable-stages.sh"
        ],
        "review": false
      },
      "verify-inherited-environment-removal": {
        "title": "Verify environment removals in the launched child",
        "text": "When changing process environment setup, seed a variable in the parent test environment, remove it in Kash, and assert its absence inside an external child on both the native and WASM hosts. The evaluator can report the variable as absent while a backend still inherits the original value.",
        "children": [],
        "sources": [
          "src/go/kame/cmd/kame/execution_native_test.go",
          "src/go/kame/host/posix/topology.go",
          "src/js/kame.js",
          "plan-055-phase-4.md"
        ],
        "review": false
      },
      "verify-job-limit-across-host-waits": {
        "title": "Verify build job limits across host waits",
        "text": "When changing the build job limit, use a single-slot build with two independent recipes that suspend on host work. Assert from process events that one recipe exits before the next starts. Also exercise a recipe that waits for a dynamically discovered producer and then runs a pipeline. It must finish with one slot. Run the discovered-build path on native and WASM. When changing WASM CLI option forwarding, check both the discovered-build and explicit-run descriptors because each carries its own job limit.",
        "children": [],
        "sources": [
          "src/go/kame/program/jobs.go",
          "src/go/kame/host/wasm/build_sources.go",
          "src/js/kame.js",
          "tests/T006-08-runtime-sequenced-inputs.sh"
        ],
        "review": false
      },
      "verify-missing-module-source-observations": {
        "title": "Verify module watch dependencies for missing and inactive sources",
        "text": "When changing authored module lookup, verify what happens when a previously missing candidate appears and when an existing source changes. A missing candidate is a valid source-host completion and must be observed for proof revalidation. For a conditional include, select a branch to load its source, switch away, and edit the now-inactive file. Verify that watch stays idle during the edit and reads the edited file when the branch is selected again. This sequence exercises previously retained source state. For an imported declaration-time constructor, add an unrelated missing import to the fixture and verify registration succeeds while still tracking the sources the constructor reaches transitively. An eager static edge to the missing import would block registration. Keep these checks on the compiler source-loading path so they do not depend on general read grants.",
        "children": [],
        "sources": [
          "src/go/kame/lang/eval/module_import.go",
          "src/go/kame/lang/eval/kash_module.go",
          "src/go/kame/cmd/kame/execution_native_test.go",
          "plan-055-phase-4.md",
          "docs/spec/050-kash-libraries.md",
          "src/go/kame/host/wasm/request_test.go",
          "plan-055.md"
        ],
        "review": false
      },
      "verify-mixed-recipe-diagnostic-provenance": {
        "title": "Remap synthetic recipe frames independently",
        "text": "A Kash recipe failure can already have an authored primary location while an enclosing call still has recipe-local offsets. Remap each synthetic frame using the rule’s source origin, including rules authored in scoped includes. In a cross-file JSON diagnostic test, assert the primary span and each frame against its own source text. For nested or generated declarations, check that iteration frames retain binding names and ordinals without exposing captured values.",
        "children": [],
        "sources": [
          "src/go/kame/program/recipe_settings.go",
          "src/go/kame/program/declaration_blocks.go",
          "src/go/kame/program/generated_rule_values.go",
          "src/go/kame/program/producer_diagnostics.go",
          "tests/T011-04-diag-language-boundaries.sh"
        ],
        "review": false
      },
      "verify-prebuilt-harness-artifact-inputs": {
        "title": "Verify artifact prerequisites for prebuilt harness runs",
        "text": "When a harness fixture uses additional build or release artifacts, check every Kamefile task that runs it with `KAME_TEST_PREBUILT`. The harness skips its prebuild in that mode, so a populated local `dist` can hide missing task prerequisites. Compare the fixture's artifact inputs with each caller's declared dependencies, and verify the task's input plan or run it in a clean workspace.",
        "children": [],
        "sources": [
          "Kamefile",
          "tests/harness.sh",
          "tests/T015-04-dist-signed-release.sh"
        ],
        "review": false
      },
      "verify-process-environment-freshness": {
        "title": "Verify recipe reuse and reruns through primary builds",
        "text": "Use the primary build invocation when proving recipe freshness. Explicit do run recipe execution requests forced runs and cannot establish a warm cache hit. A primary build with explicit grants can still take the unified runner path. If that fixture needs to prove cache reuse without data input, supply terminal stdin through a PTY so an inherited nonterminal descriptor does not become an opaque input. Count actual recipe executions as well as checking output, since identical output can come from either a cache hit or a needless rerun. To prove downstream invalidation, choose an upstream edit that changes the bytes of the intermediate artifact and verify that the consumer reruns. A comment-only C edit may rerun compilation while leaving object bytes unchanged and allowing the link step to reuse its result.\n\nWhen testing process environment freshness, first vary ambient values hidden by unit or stage overrides and removals and check that the build is reused. Then change a value inherited by the child and check that the build reruns.",
        "children": [],
        "sources": [
          "src/go/kame/cmd/kame/execution_native_test.go",
          "plan-055-phase-4.md",
          "docs/spec/051-kash-session-authority.md",
          "tests/T017-17-eval-process-expressions.sh",
          "tests/T006-10-runtime-environment-watch.sh",
          "tests/recipe-settings.py",
          "tests/kash-recipes.py",
          "docs/skills/kame/ref/make-migration.md",
          "docs/skills/kame/ref/rules.md",
          "plan-055.md",
          "tests/T006-06-runtime-environment.sh",
          "docs/spec/048-kash-practical-scripting.md"
        ],
        "review": false
      },
      "verify-pure-operation-registry-coverage": {
        "title": "Exercise each registered pure operation with host requests enabled",
        "text": "When adding or reclassifying a pure operation, add a working example to TestPureOperations. The inventory checks which operations actually run against the registry, including operations reached inside examples. Keep direct host requests and the usual effect grants enabled so the test can detect implicit host requests, dependency observations, effects, or publication attempts.",
        "children": [],
        "sources": [
          "src/go/kame/operations/test/operations.go#TestPureOperations",
          "src/go/kame/operations/registry.go",
          "plan-055.md"
        ],
        "review": false
      },
      "verify-quiescence-after-latest-watch-idle": {
        "title": "Wait for the latest watch cycle before asserting quiescence",
        "text": "Before asserting that a watch log stays unchanged, parse only complete JSON lines and wait until the latest event is watch-idle for at least the required cycle. Also verify the expected output and that the watcher is alive. An earlier idle event can coexist with visible writes from an active later cycle. Read the log through a separate handle so the test does not move the writer's file offset.",
        "children": [],
        "sources": [
          "tests/computed-paths.py",
          "tests/T038-05-lang-computed-paths.sh",
          "plan-055.md"
        ],
        "review": false
      },
      "verify-service-restart-readiness-per-generation": {
        "title": "Test readiness for each service generation",
        "text": "When testing whether a dependent resumes after a service health restart, trigger the first health failure only after the dependent has started. If readiness is represented by a filesystem marker, retire the old marker before reporting failure and have the replacement publish its own readiness. Verify that the dependent starts again after replacement readiness. A stale marker can let the readiness probe succeed before the replacement process starts.",
        "children": [],
        "sources": [
          "src/go/kame/program/test/runtime_execution.go#TestServiceHealthFailureRestartsAndResumesConsumer",
          "plan-055.md"
        ],
        "review": false
      },
      "verify-standalone-wasm-process-evaluation": {
        "title": "Verify process evaluation through the standalone WASM API",
        "text": "When changing WASM evaluation of process expressions, exercise KameProgram.evaluate directly. The standalone evaluator needs the shared host tool-resolution request and portable capture default even when no CLI session is compiled. Test captured stdout, inspected exit status, a lazily evaluated definition, caller cwd, missing-tool fallback, and denial without run grants.\n\nWhen running `tests/wasm/test-memory-reuse.mjs` against a freshly built module, set `KAME_WASM` to that module's path. The script selects the binary from that variable or falls back to `build/wasm/kame.wasm`. Positional arguments have no effect on binary selection.\n\nWhen changing source-backed evaluation in the JavaScript API, compile with an absolute source name and test `KameProgram.evaluate` against a relative authored import. Vary the evaluation cwd independently, verify the imported module retains its own file provenance, and check that a general read of the same file is denied when compile grants only scoped source access. A separately parsed evaluation AST needs the compiled program’s lexical source origin alongside its expression diagnostic location.",
        "children": [],
        "sources": [
          "src/go/kame/host/wasm/runtime.go",
          "src/go/kame/host/wasm/session.go",
          "src/go/kame/lang/eval/tool.go",
